      "detail": "8th in Atlantic Division",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "Another Leafs season crumbled into the Atlantic Division's dustbin - 32-36-14, eighth place, 78 points, a yawning 31 games behind the leaders, capped by a soul-crushing <strong>L7 skid</strong> that buried any faint playoff whispers. This wasn't just missing the dance; it was a masterclass in mediocrity, the core four - Matthews, Marner, Nylander, Tavares - grinding out points that evaporated under defensive sieves and goaltending roulette. No heroic rallies, no late magic. Just the cold math of another early summer in Toronto. Whispers of a <strong>coaching shakeup</strong> echo from the sidelines, with brass eyeing a fresh voice to jolt this stagnant roster, while free agency looms thin and the draft board offers no first-round salvation without bold trades. The real storyline? Captaincy questions swirl around Auston Matthews, whose Herculean shifts couldn't mask the team's systemic rot - 50-goal seasons mean squat without blue-line bite. Veterans like me have seen this script a dozen times: promise, pressure, purge. <strong>Watch the lottery draw Tuesday - a top-5 pick could rewrite this franchise's haunted offseason narrative, or it's Groundhog Day all over again.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 14, 2026"
      },
