    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "19-24",
      "detail": "4th in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "Daulton Varsho's bat erupted like a long-dormant volcano - a <strong>walk-off grand slam in the 10th inning</strong> that silenced the Rays and snapped Toronto's spiral, turning a potential sweep into a gritty 5-3 escape at Rogers Centre. The Blue Jays, mired at 19-24 and <strong>9.5 games back</strong> in the AL East's fourth-place muck, desperately needed this explosion - their first such homer since Steve Pearce's blast nine years back - after dropping the series opener 7-6 and the rubber match 8-5 to Tampa's buzzsaw. Varsho, channeling the ghosts of Jays' glory, loaded the bases with sheer Jays' stubbornness and crushed it 421 feet, the biggest swing in a season of small frustrations. No ABS controversy or ninth-inning heartburn could steal the thunder this time. At 4th in the East with playoff seed 11 staring them down, this W1 streak feels like a flicker, not a fire - but in a dugout that's seen 43 losses already, it's oxygen. The real test looms <strong>Fri 5/15 at the Tigers</strong>, where momentum either builds or crumbles against Detroit's resurgent arms; win there, and whispers of contention return - lose, and it's back to the grind.",
        "updated": "Updated May 14, 2026"
      },
